1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:02,750
Two thousand three
hundred switches.

2
00:00:03,710 --> 00:00:05,890
That's all the first
computer chip you could buy

3
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:06,690
had.

4
00:00:07,030 --> 00:00:10,030
The chips we use now have
far more, and the real

5
00:00:10,030 --> 00:00:11,630
number is hard to even picture.

6
00:00:12,430 --> 00:00:16,250
That first chip was
Intel's 4004, from 1971.

7
00:00:17,290 --> 00:00:20,390
Each of its switches, called
a transistor, is simply

8
00:00:20,390 --> 00:00:21,370
on or off.

9
00:00:22,130 --> 00:00:25,290
Say you tried to count them,
one every second, without

10
00:00:25,290 --> 00:00:26,090
ever sleeping.

11
00:00:26,810 --> 00:00:28,870
Two thousand three hundred
would take you under

12
00:00:28,870 --> 00:00:29,670
an hour.

13
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:32,490
A billion would take
you over thirty years.

14
00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:35,710
Even an everyday laptop
chip has billions.

15
00:00:36,690 --> 00:00:40,230
And the biggest yet,
Apple's M2 Ultra from 2023,

16
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:43,830
has a hundred and thirty-four
billion, about fifty-eight

17
00:00:43,830 --> 00:00:45,370
million times the first.

18
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:48,890
Counting those would take
you more than four thousand

19
00:00:48,890 --> 00:00:49,690
years.
